web-game-snake.prompt:
Use the Phosphor template to build a snake mini-game that runs by opening a single file.

Template: the frontend-design skill ships this template as a reference implementation. Read its SKILL.md first, then read templates/web-game-snake.html and templates/web-game-snake.info.md as described in the case-template section, and treat them as the baseline for layout skeleton, color roles, type hierarchy, and interaction.

Gameplay and rules:
- Arrow keys and WASD steer, on-screen direction buttons cover touch, and diagonal input is not accepted;
- Eating food grows the snake and raises the score, and hitting a wall or the snake itself ends the run so the player can restart straight away;
- Pause is supported and the scrim stays translucent so the board is still visible;
- Recent runs are kept in local storage, and changing storageKey clears them.

Content to replace:
- Rewrite the title, subtitle, button labels, and overlay copy in copy;
- rules exposes cols, rows, cell, startLength, and three speed steps, and a larger cell widens the board;
- In copy.keysHint, wrap key names in double brackets such as [[Space]] and the render layer turns them into keycaps;
- theme.food is the food color and has to stay distinguishable from the snake.

Design goal:
Keep the visual language of the template: the near-black olive ground, one mint accent, monospace type, the square-cell board, the snake head with eyes, and the diamond food. Adjust hue and type within that language, but do not switch to a black-and-neon pixel look, and do not add rounded candy-colored cards.

web-game-2048.prompt:
Use the slate ladder template to build a 2048 number-merging game that runs by opening a single file.

Template: the frontend-design skill ships this template as a reference implementation. Read its SKILL.md first, then read templates/web-game-2048.html and templates/web-game-2048.info.md as described in the case-template section, and treat them as the baseline for layout skeleton, color roles, type hierarchy, and interaction.

Gameplay and rules:
- The board is fixed at 4x4, arrow keys, WASD, and swiping on the board all move the tiles, and equal tiles merge into a doubled value;
- After each move a new 2 or 4 appears in an empty cell, and the run ends when no move is possible;
- Keep the single-step undo, and the button greys out once it is used;
- The best score is kept in local storage, and changing storageKey clears it.

Content to replace:
- Rewrite the title, subtitle, both buttons, the end-screen copy, and the rules lines in copy;
- rules exposes storageKey, startTiles, and winValue;
- tiles is the value-to-color ladder, covering 2 through 2048 with a beyond fallback, and the keys must not skip values;
- Every step's bg and fg pair has to pass WCAG AA, so check each one after changing colors.

Design goal:
Keep the visual language of the template: the slate ground, one cobalt accent, the two score readouts at the top, the ladder strip under the board, and rounding only on the tiles. Adjust hue within that language, but the ladder has to stay one hue pushing through lightness, and the board must not fall back to beige with candy-colored tiles.

